# The repository root .gitignore has no Terraform section, so this file is load-bearing
# rather than decoration. Removing it means the first `terraform init` commits a ~700 MB
# provider binary, and the first `terraform apply` commits a state file containing every
# value the root module manages.

# Provider binaries and module cache. Large, platform-specific, and reconstructible from
# .terraform.lock.hcl.
.terraform/
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# State. NEVER commit this. Until the azurerm backend is bootstrapped and uncommented (see
# versions.tf), `terraform apply` writes state here in plaintext — and this root manages
# Container App secret values, so a committed state file is a committed credential.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Real variable values. The committed example is terraform.tfvars.example.
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json
!terraform.tfvars.example

# Saved plans are binary and contain the full resource graph including secret values.
*.tfplan
tfplan
plan.out

# Backend configuration passed with -backend-config, which names a storage account.
*.backend.hcl
backend.hcl

# .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. It is the file that makes provider
# versions reproducible and it belongs in the repository.
